{0 Tutorial}

This tutorial makes two components. The first is a counter. The second is a
component that asks the server for a greeting with a Remote call.

The code of this page is in [docs/examples/] of the repository. Dune compiles
it for the server (native) and for the browser (Melange). A test makes sure
that the code on this page is the same as those files, and runs the examples
on this page.

{1 Part 1: a counter}

{2 The machine}

A component is a machine and a view. The machine holds all the behaviour. The
view shows the state of the machine and sends events to it.

The states of a machine are the constructors of a plain OCaml variant. The
counter has one state, [Count], which carries an [int]. The function [name]
gives the name of the state of a snapshot: here always ["count"].
[Machine.make ~name] uses it to know when the machine enters a new state; the
checker and the spec show the names.

The machine has two events. It does not name them: the checker and the spec
label each event by its cause, such as [press button "Increment"] (see
{!page-machines.events}).
The update function gets the state and the event,
and gives the next step. [Increment] goes to [Count] with the number plus one.
[Decrement] goes to [Count] with the number minus one, and stays when the
number is 0. The view is written in mlx, the JSX syntax for OCaml.

{@mlx file=examples/shared/tutorial_counter.mlx[
open Silica

type event = Increment | Decrement
type state = Count of int

let name (Count _) = "count"

let machine =
  Machine.make ~id:"counter" ~init:(Count 0) ~name (fun (Count n) event ->
      match event with
      | Increment ->
          Go (Count (n + 1))
      | Decrement when n = 0 ->
          Stay []
      | Decrement ->
          Go (Count (n - 1))
  )

let view (Count n) : event Node.t =
  <div>
    <button on_press=Decrement disabled=(n = 0)>"Decrement"</button>
    <span>(Node.text (string_of_int n))</span>
    <button on_press=Increment>"Increment"</button>
  </div>

let component = Component.make machine view
]}

{2 Run the machine}

A machine is a plain value. You can start it and send events to it without a
browser. [Machine.start] gives the first state and the actions that the host
must run. [Machine.step] gives the next state, the actions, and how the step
changed the state. A machine made with [Machine.make] has no props, so both
take no props.

{@ocaml[
# let counter = Tutorial_counter.machine;;
val counter :
  (unit, Tutorial_counter.state, Tutorial_counter.event) Silica.Machine.t =
  <abstr>
# let state, _ = Silica.Machine.start counter;;
val state : Tutorial_counter.state = Tutorial_counter.Count 0
# let state, _, _ =
    Silica.Machine.step counter state Tutorial_counter.Increment;;
val state : Tutorial_counter.state = Tutorial_counter.Count 1
]}

[Render.to_string] makes the HTML of a view. The server uses it for the first
paint of a page.

{@ocaml[
# Silica.Render.to_string (Tutorial_counter.view state);;
- : string =
"<div><button>Decrement</button><span>1</span><button>Increment</button></div>"
]}

{2 Check the machine}

The checker ([silica.check]) runs every event in every state that it can
reach, and checks properties of each view. For example, each button must have
a name that a screen reader can read. The counter can count forever, so
[within] limits the search to the numbers 0 to 3.

{@ocaml[
# let within (Tutorial_counter.Count n) = n <= 3;;
val within : Tutorial_counter.state -> bool = <fun>
# let report = Check.run (Check.component ~within Tutorial_counter.component) in
  if Check.passed report then Printf.sprintf "%d states, no problem" report.states
  else Format.asprintf "%a" Check.pp_report report;;
- : string = "4 states, no problem"
]}

{2 Show it in the browser}

The browser package [silica.browser] mounts a component into an element of the
page. The {{!section-browser}second part} shows a complete client file.

{1:part2 Part 2: a machine with a Remote call}

{2 The endpoint}

A Remote call is one request to a typed endpoint on the server. Declare the
endpoint in code that the server and the browser share. The codecs change the
input and the output to JSON and back.

{@ocaml file=examples/shared/tutorial_api.ml[
let greeting =
  Silica.Remote.declare ~path:"/api/greeting" ~input:Silica.Codec.string
    ~output:Silica.Codec.string ~rejected:Silica.Codec.string
]}

[rejected] is the codec of the application error: the handler on the server
can answer [Error reason], for example when the input is not valid.

{2 The machine}

The greeting machine has four states. In [Idle], a press on the button sends
[Ask], and the machine goes to [Asking]. The call runs during [Asking]: the
[during] function gives the call for [Asking] and nothing for the other states.
The call starts when the machine enters the state, and its answer comes back
as the event [Answered]. If the machine leaves the state first, the answer is
dropped.

The answer is a {!Silica.Remote.answer}: [Ok text] takes the machine to
[Shown]. [Rejected reason] (the handler answered [Error reason]) and
[Failed error] (no answer, or an answer that does not decode) take it to
[Failed]. {!Silica.Remote.error_to_string} gives a sentence for the error.

The update ends with [| _ -> Ignore]: the machine does not handle the other
events in the other states. The checker reports a button whose event the
machine ignores, so this catch-all cannot hide a mistake.

{@mlx file=examples/shared/tutorial_greeting.mlx[
open Silica

type event = Ask | Answered of (string, string) Remote.answer
type state = Idle | Asking | Shown of string | Failed of string

let name = function
  | Idle ->
      "idle"
  | Asking ->
      "asking"
  | Shown _ ->
      "shown"
  | Failed _ ->
      "failed"

let machine =
  Machine.make ~id:"greeting" ~init:Idle ~name
    ~during:(function
      | Asking ->
          [
            Remote.call Tutorial_api.greeting "Ada" (fun answer ->
                Answered answer
            );
          ]
      | Idle | Shown _ | Failed _ ->
          []
      )
    (fun state event ->
      match (state, event) with
      | (Idle | Shown _ | Failed _), Ask ->
          Go Asking
      | Asking, Answered (Ok text) ->
          Go (Shown text)
      | Asking, Answered (Rejected reason) ->
          Go (Failed reason)
      | Asking, Answered (Failed error) ->
          Go (Failed (Remote.error_to_string error))
      | _ ->
          Ignore
    )

let view : state -> event Node.t = function
  | Idle ->
      <button on_press=Ask>"Say hello"</button>
  | Asking ->
      <p>"Asking the server."</p>
  | Shown text ->
      <div>
        <p>(Node.text text)</p> <button on_press=Ask>"Ask again"</button>
      </div>
  | Failed reason ->
      <div>
        <p>(Node.text reason)</p> <button on_press=Ask>"Try again"</button>
      </div>

let component = Component.make machine view
]}

{2 Run the machine}

When the machine enters [Asking], [Machine.step] gives a [Start_call] action.
The host runs the call. The test below does not run it, so the machine stays in
[Asking].

{@ocaml[
# let greeting = Tutorial_greeting.machine;;
val greeting :
  (unit, Tutorial_greeting.state, Tutorial_greeting.event) Silica.Machine.t =
  <abstr>
# let state, _ = Silica.Machine.start greeting;;
val state : Tutorial_greeting.state = Tutorial_greeting.Idle
# let state, actions, _ =
    Silica.Machine.step greeting state Tutorial_greeting.Ask;;
val state : Tutorial_greeting.state = Tutorial_greeting.Asking
val actions : Tutorial_greeting.event Silica.Action.host list =
  [Silica.Action.Start_call
    {Silica.Action.id = "greeting/asking//api/greeting \"Ada\"";
     endpoint = <abstr>; input = <poly>; timeout_ms = None;
     on_result = <fun>}]
# Silica.Machine.active greeting state;;
- : Silica.Machine.resource list =
[Silica.Machine.Call
  {Silica.Machine.id = "greeting/asking//api/greeting \"Ada\"";
   path = "/api/greeting"}]
]}

The machine gets the answer as an event, as any other event:

{@ocaml[
# let state, _, _ =
    Silica.Machine.step greeting state
      (Tutorial_greeting.Answered (Ok "Hello, Ada"));;
val state : Tutorial_greeting.state = Tutorial_greeting.Shown "Hello, Ada"
# Silica.Render.to_string (Tutorial_greeting.view state);;
- : string = "<div><p>Hello, Ada</p><button>Ask again</button></div>"
]}

{2 Check the machine}

The checker gives the call each answer in [answers], and also the errors
[Failed (Http 500)] and [Failed (Network "down")]. So it checks the success
path and the error paths.

{@ocaml[
# let report =
    Check.run
      (Check.component
         ~samples:[ Check.answers Tutorial_api.greeting [ "Hello, Ada" ] ]
         Tutorial_greeting.component)
  in
  if Check.passed report then Printf.sprintf "%d states, no problem" report.states
  else Format.asprintf "%a" Check.pp_report report;;
- : string = "5 states, no problem"
]}

{2:browser The browser}

The client file mounts the component into the element with the id
[silica-root]. The mount returns the app, which the page could stop with
[Silica_browser.send app Stop]. Dune compiles it with Melange, and a bundler such as esbuild
makes the file for the page.

{@ocaml file=examples/client/tutorial_client.ml[
let app =
  Silica_browser.mount
    ~root:(Silica_browser.by_id "silica-root")
    Tutorial_greeting.component
]}

{2 The server}

The server answers the endpoint and renders the page. [silica new] writes a
complete server for a new app (see {!page-install.new}). See
{!page-recipes.hosting} for the file, and {!page-server} for what the server
does.

